\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
In this section, we describe our proposed method, Iterative Dynamic Region Refinement with Geometry-Aware Reconstruction (IDRR-GAR), which is designed to improve self-supervised monocular depth estimation in dynamic scenes. IDRR-GAR is built upon a dual-stream architecture that decouples static and dynamic depth estimation and integrates an iterative refinement process with geometry-aware sampling. This design helps overcome challenges posed by non-rigid motion and scale ambiguity while ensuring reliable depth cues and temporal consistency.

\subsection{Dual-Stream Initialization}
In the first stage, our framework obtains initial depth estimates separately for static and dynamic regions. In the static stream, an unsupervised depth estimator produces reliable depth maps and motion cues for rigid regions. Simultaneously, an object network processes dynamic regions and yields coarse depth predictions based on available motion information. By decoupling these two streams, the accurate static depth estimates serve as effective scale references, which later guide the refinement process of the dynamic depth predictions.

\subsection{Geometry-Aware Sampling}
Dynamic regions, especially those affected by non-rigid motion, often exhibit high uncertainty. To selectively focus on these challenging areas, we incorporate a geometry-aware sampling module inspired by the GPS module in IMRNet. Given an intermediate feature map \(F \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C \times H \times W}\), we compute the per-pixel uncertainty as
\begin{equation}
    U(i,j) = \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} \left( \left| \frac{\partial F_{c}(i,j)}{\partial x} \right| + \left| \frac{\partial F_{c}(i,j)}{\partial y} \right| \right),
\end{equation}
where \(\frac{\partial F_{c}(i,j)}{\partial x}\) and \(\frac{\partial F_{c}(i,j)}{\partial y}\) denote the spatial gradients of the feature map along the $x$ and $y$ axes, respectively. The top-$k$ pixels with the highest uncertainty are selected as target regions for further refinement. This strategy reduces the reliance on potentially noisy object masks and directs the refinement module to focus on the most critical areas in the dynamic regions.

\subsection{Iterative Dynamic Region Refinement}
The core innovation of IDRR-GAR is its iterative refinement of dynamic depth predictions using a transformer-based encoder--decoder module. Instead of performing a one-shot estimation, our method progressively refines the initial dynamic depth predictions. This approach not only better handles non-rigid motion, but also improves scale alignment as static cues and geometry-aware sampling are incorporated at every refinement iteration. The iterative process is summarized in Algorithm~1.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Iterative Depth Refinement in Dynamic Regions}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Initial dynamic depth map $D_{dyn}^{(0)}$, input image $I$, maximum iterations $T$
\For{$t=1$ \textbf{to} $T$}
    \State Extract features: $F \leftarrow \mathrm{Encoder}(I)$
    \State Compute refinement delta: $\Delta D^{(t)} \leftarrow \mathrm{TransformerRefine}(I, D_{dyn}^{(t-1)})$
    \State Update depth: $D_{dyn}^{(t)} \leftarrow D_{dyn}^{(t-1)} + \Delta D^{(t)}$
    \State Adjust scale using static cues and geometry-aware sampled regions
    \State Compute loss components (photometric, smoothness, and scale alignment)
\EndFor
\State \textbf{Output:} Refined dynamic depth map $D_{dyn}^{(T)}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The training process incorporates a composite loss function that jointly enforces image reconstruction quality, depth smoothness, and correct scale alignment. The individual loss components are defined as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Photometric Loss:} Given a predicted depth map and the reconstructed image $I_{rec}$ (obtained by warping a source view), we employ an $L_{1}$ norm:
    \begin{equation}
        L_{photo} = \| I - I_{rec} \|_{1}.
    \end{equation}
    \item \textbf{Smoothness Loss:} To encourage spatial coherence in the depth map, we penalize abrupt spatial variations:
    \begin{equation}
        L_{smooth} = \frac{1}{HW} \sum_{i,j} \Big(|D(i,j) - D(i+1,j)| + |D(i,j) - D(i,j+1)|\Big).
    \end{equation}
    \item \textbf{Scale Alignment Loss:} To mitigate scale ambiguity, we compute a scale factor
    \begin{equation}
        s = \frac{\mathrm{median}(D_{gt})}{\mathrm{median}(D_{pred})+\epsilon},
    \end{equation}
    and define the loss as
    \begin{equation}
        L_{scale} = \| s \cdot D_{pred} - D_{gt} \|_{1}.
    \end{equation}
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Integration and End-to-End Training}
The final depth estimation network is trained in an integrated, end-to-end fashion, combining the loss terms from both the static and dynamic streams. The overall loss function is formulated as

\begin{equation}
    L_{total} = L_{photo} + \lambda_{smooth} \cdot L_{smooth} + \lambda_{scale} \cdot L_{scale} + \lambda_{iter} \cdot L_{iter},
\end{equation}

\noindent where $L_{iter}$ denotes the cumulative loss over all refinement iterations, and $\lambda_{smooth}$, $\lambda_{scale}$, and $\lambda_{iter}$ are hyperparameters that control the relative weight of each loss component. Through this integrated framework, and by processing sequential frames with transformer-based attention, our design naturally enforces temporal consistency---an essential quality for accurately handling the non-rigid motion observed in dynamic scenes.

\subsection{Contributions}
The primary contributions of the proposed IDRR-GAR framework can be summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Stream Depth Estimation:} Implements a decoupled approach for static and dynamic regions, enabling accurate static depth estimates to serve as a reliable scale reference for dynamic region refinement.
    \item \textbf{Geometry-Aware Sampling:} Introduces an uncertainty-driven sampling strategy to automatically target areas with high geometric complexity, mitigating the dependency on imperfect object masks.
    \item \textbf{Iterative Refinement with Transformer Attention:} Leverages a transformer-based encoder--decoder architecture in an iterative framework to progressively refine dynamic depth predictions, effectively handling non-rigid motion.
    \item \textbf{Progressive Scale Alignment:} Integrates static cues and geometry-aware sampling within each refinement iteration to progressively reduce scale ambiguity inherent in monocular depth estimation.
\end{itemize}

In summary, the IDRR-GAR framework fuses the advantages of dual-stream depth estimation with an iterative refinement strategy driven by transformer attention and geometry-aware sampling. This holistic design enhances depth estimation accuracy in dynamic scenes that are characterized by complex, non-rigid motions, thereby advancing the state-of-the-art in self-supervised monocular depth estimation.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental}
%% Experimental Configuration and Evaluation

In this section, we describe the experimental configuration used to evaluate the proposed Iterative Dynamic Region Refinement with Geometry-Aware Reconstruction (IDRR-GAR) method against the baseline approach from \citet{2404.14908v1}. Our evaluation focuses on both static and dynamic scenes and quantitatively measures improvements in photometric reconstruction, temporal consistency, and scale alignment. In addition, an ablation study is conducted to assess the impact of the iterative refinement module as well as the benefits of geometry-aware sampling.

\subsection{Data Preparation and Preprocessing}
Benchmark datasets such as Cityscapes and KITTI are employed for our experiments. These datasets provide a diverse set of scenes with both rigid (static) and non-rigid (dynamic) motions. Data preprocessing is implemented using a PyTorch DataLoader in conjunction with \texttt{torchvision} transforms. All input images are resized to $256\times320$ and converted to tensors; ground truth depth maps (when available for evaluation) are processed in the same manner. An example implementation is provided below:

\begin{verbatim}
import torch
from torch.utils.data import Dataset, DataLoader
from torchvision import transforms

class DynamicScenesDataset(Dataset):
    def __init__(self, data_paths, transform=None):
        self.data_paths = data_paths
        self.transform = transform

    def __len__(self):
        return len(self.data_paths)

    def __getitem__(self, idx):
        image = load_image(self.data_paths[idx]['image'])
        gt_depth = load_depth(self.data_paths[idx]['depth'])
        if self.transform:
            image = self.transform(image)
        return image, gt_depth

transform = transforms.Compose([
    transforms.Resize((256, 320)),
    transforms.ToTensor()
])

dataset = DynamicScenesDataset(data_paths, transform=transform)

dataloader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=16, shuffle=True, num_workers=4)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Model Architectures and Loss Formulations}
The baseline \textbf{BaseDepthModel} employs a simple encoder--decoder architecture for unsupervised monocular depth estimation. The proposed \textbf{IDRR-GARModel} builds upon this network by adding an iterative refinement loop and a geometry-aware sampling module. The main contributions of our method are summarized below:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Stream Initialization:} Two parallel streams produce reliable depth estimates for static regions and coarse depth maps for dynamic regions.
    \item \textbf{Geometry-Aware Sampling:} Dynamic regions are prioritized using uncertainty metrics computed from gradient magnitudes in the feature maps.
    \item \textbf{Iterative Dynamic Region Refinement:} A transformer-inspired module computes residual corrections ($\delta$) that are added iteratively to the initial depth predictions.
    \item \textbf{Progressive Scale Alignment:} A custom loss aligns the scale of the predicted depth with reference cues from static regions.
\end{itemize}

A simplified pseudocode for the forward pass in the proposed model is presented below:

\begin{verbatim}
class IDRRGARModel(BaseDepthModel):
    def __init__(self, iterations=3):
        super(IDRRGARModel, self).__init__()
        self.iterations = iterations
        self.refine_conv = nn.Conv2d(4, 1, kernel_size=3, padding=1)

    def transformer_refine(self, input_image, depth_est):
        # Concatenate the input image with the repeated depth estimate
        x = torch.cat([input_image, depth_est.repeat(3, 1, 1, 1)], dim=1)
        delta = self.refine_conv(x)
        return delta

    def forward(self, input_image):
        depth_est = super(IDRRGARModel, self).forward(input_image)
        for i in range(self.iterations):
            delta = self.transformer_refine(input_image, depth_est)
            depth_est = depth_est + delta
        return depth_est
\end{verbatim}

During training, the following loss functions are employed:

\begin{equation}
L_{photo} = \|D_{pred} - D_{gt}\|_{1}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
L_{smooth} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i,j} \Big(|D_{i,j} - D_{i+1,j}| + |D_{i,j} - D_{i,j+1}|\Big)
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
L_{scale} = \left\| \frac{\mathrm{median}(D_{gt})}{\mathrm{median}(D_{pred})} \cdot D_{pred} - D_{gt} \right\|_{1}
\end{equation}

The total loss is defined as follows:

\begin{equation}
L = L_{photo} + \lambda_{smooth} \; L_{smooth} + \lambda_{scale} \; L_{scale},
\end{equation}

with \(\lambda_{smooth} = \lambda_{scale} = 0.1\).

\subsection{Training Procedure}
Both the baseline and IDRR-GAR models are trained under identical conditions using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of \(10^{-3}\). The training procedure is outlined in Algorithm~1.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Training Procedure for Depth Estimation Models}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} DataLoader \(\mathcal{D}\), number of epochs $E$, models $M_{base}$ and $M_{idrr}$
\For{epoch $= 1$ to $E$}
    \For{each batch $(I, D_{gt}) \in \mathcal{D}$}
        \State \textbf{// Baseline Model Forward Pass}
        \State $D_{pred}^{base} = M_{base}(I)$
        \State Compute $L_{base} = L_{photo}(D_{pred}^{base}, D_{gt}) + 0.1\,L_{smooth} + 0.1\,L_{scale}$
        \State Backpropagate and update $M_{base}$
        \State 
        \State \textbf{// IDRR-GAR Forward Pass}
        \State $D_{pred}^{idrr} = M_{idrr}(I)$
        \State Compute $L_{idrr} = L_{photo}(D_{pred}^{idrr}, D_{gt}) + 0.1\,L_{smooth} + 0.1\,L_{scale}$
        \State Backpropagate and update $M_{idrr}$
    \EndFor
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

For the ablation study, we train a variant of IDRR-GAR with a single iteration (i.e., without iterative refinement). Region-specific losses are further computed by incorporating geometry-aware sampling into the training process.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics and Statistical Analysis}

Performance is evaluated using standard metrics for monocular depth estimation:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Absolute Relative Error (AbsRel)}
    \item \textbf{Squared Relative Error (SqRel)}
    \item \textbf{Accuracy (\(\delta < 1.25\))}
\end{itemize}

Temporal consistency is quantified by computing the variance of depth predictions across consecutive frames. To assess the statistical significance of the observed improvements between IDRR-GAR and the baseline, a paired t-test is performed using \texttt{scipy.stats}. For example:

\begin{verbatim}
from scipy import stats
errors_idrr = np.array(losses_idrr)
errors_base = np.array(losses_base)

t_stat, p_value = stats.ttest_rel(errors_idrr, errors_base)
print(f"Paired t-test: t-statistic = {t_stat:.3f}, p-value = {p_value:.3f}")
\end{verbatim}

This experimental framework enables a rigorous evaluation of the overall performance of the proposed IDRR-GAR method, quantifies the contribution of its iterative refinement module, and demonstrates the effectiveness of geometry-aware sampling in addressing challenges associated with dynamic scenes.

